---
title: "Worked example: family CEOs and CEO pay"
description: >
  A complete sensitivity analysis with bootmakr in R and Stata: the four-stage
  workflow of Lonati and Wulff (2026), applied to Chen, Chittoor and Vissa (2021).
---

```{r helpers, include = FALSE}
source(file.path(getOption("bootmakr.precompute.dir"), "helpers.R"))
```

This article goes through a complete sensitivity analysis with `bootmakr`, from
the first look at the standard errors to the check that the bootstrap can be
trusted. It follows the four stages recommended by Lonati and Wulff (2026) and
reproduces the empirical illustration of that paper. The reason a bootstrap
is needed at all, that the ITCV and the analytic interval of `sensemakr`
break down with robust standard errors, is shown in
[Why bootstrap?](why-bootstrap.html).

Every step is shown in R and in Stata. Pick a language on any tab and the rest
of the page follows.

## The study

Chen, Chittoor, and Vissa (2021) ask whether CEOs who belong to the family that
controls the firm are paid more than professional CEOs. In Model 2 of their
Table 2 they regress the natural logarithm of total CEO pay on a family-CEO
indicator and a set of controls: CEO tenure and education, firm sales, firm
age, ownership shares, and year and industry indicators. The data cover 277
Indian firms observed over several years, 2,011 firm-year observations in all,
and the standard errors are clustered by firm. The coefficient of the family-CEO
indicator is 0.33 (*p* = .001): family CEOs earn roughly 39 percent more.

The authors themselves point out that the data are observational and that no
natural experiment or instrument is available. CEO ability, family dynamics or
firm strategy could drive both who becomes CEO and what the CEO is paid. The
question for a sensitivity analysis is how strong such an omitted variable
would have to be before the conclusion changes.

## Data and model

The authors share their data in an
[OSF repository](https://osf.io/bsv2e/?view_only=885d1ed5498c4b3c8a1194882e468286),
through the view-only link given with their article. The file can be read
from there directly; the data are theirs, and this site keeps no copy.

`r tabs_r("setup")`

```{r setup}
library(bootmakr)
library(haven)

osf  <- "https://osf.io/download/stk7y/?view_only=885d1ed5498c4b3c8a1194882e468286"
chen <- read_dta(osf)

ceo.model <- lg_ceopay ~ owner_ceo + ceo_tenure + PA_nic3_med + ceo_edu_dummy +
  lg_sales + firm_age + promoters_pct + institutions_pct +
  factor(year) + factor(nic_code_1digit)
```

`lg_ceopay` is the outcome, `owner_ceo` the family-CEO indicator, and
`co_code` identifies the firm.

`r tabs_stata("setup")`

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
cat("```stata", stata_code("first-steps", "data"), "",
    stata_code("first-steps", "controls"), "```", "", sep = "\n")
```

`copy` downloads the file to the working directory. `lg_ceopay` is the
outcome, `owner_ceo` the family-CEO indicator, and `co_code` identifies the
firm. The global `$controls` holds the control variables and the year and
industry indicators.

`r tabs_end()`

## Stages 1 and 2: which standard errors, and how different are they?

The first two stages ask whether robust standard errors are needed and, if
so, how far they are from the conventional ones. Here the answer to the first
question is yes. Family-CEO status varies mostly between firms, and
observations of the same firm are not independent, which is why Chen et al.
(2021) cluster by firm. For the second question, estimate the model both
ways.

`r tabs_r("se")`

```{r se}
library(sandwich)

fit <- lm(ceo.model, data = chen)

se <- c(conventional = sqrt(vcov(fit)["owner_ceo", "owner_ceo"]),
        clustered    = sqrt(vcovCL(fit, cluster = ~ co_code)["owner_ceo", "owner_ceo"]))

round(c(estimate = coef(fit)[["owner_ceo"]], se,
        ratio = se[["clustered"]] / se[["conventional"]]), 3)
```

`r tabs_stata("se")`

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("ceo-pay-1", "model2")
```

`r tabs_end()`

The coefficient is `r num(coef(fit)[["owner_ceo"]])`, as published. Its
cluster-robust standard error (`r num(se[["clustered"]], 3)`) is almost twice
the conventional one (`r num(se[["conventional"]], 3)`). With a gap of this
size the impact threshold of a confounding variable (ITCV) can no longer be
read as a product of correlations, because it is computed from a *t* statistic
that now rests on a different variance estimator, and the analytic interval
of `sensemakr` is off by the same factor. This is the situation `bootmakr`
is made for; see [Why bootstrap?](why-bootstrap.html).

## Stage 3: how strong is a plausible omitted variable?

An omitted variable is unobserved by definition, so its strength has to be
judged against something that is observed. `sensemakr` (Cinelli & Hazlett,
2020) does this with a *benchmark*: an observed control variable whose
explanatory power serves as the yardstick. The question becomes: what would
the coefficient be if a variable `kd` times as strong as the benchmark had
been left out? `bootmakr` computes that bias-adjusted coefficient and
bootstraps the whole procedure, resampling firms, to obtain its standard
error, confidence interval and *p*-value.

### An omitted variable as strong as CEO tenure

We take CEO tenure as the benchmark. The call needs the model, the treatment,
the benchmark and the cluster variable, nothing more.

`r tabs_r("short")`

```{r short, eval = FALSE}
ceo.sensitivity <- bootmakr(formula = ceo.model,
                            data = chen, treat = "owner_ceo",
                            benchmark_covariates = "ceo_tenure",
                            cluster = "co_code")
```

`r tabs_stata("short")`

```stata
bootmakr lg_ceopay owner_ceo $controls, ///
    treat(owner_ceo) benchmark(ceo_tenure) cluster(co_code)
```

`r tabs_end()`

By default `bootmakr` evaluates an omitted variable exactly as strong as the
benchmark (`kd = 1`) and draws 1,000 bootstrap samples. No seed is set unless
you supply one, so two runs of this call give slightly different results. For
the numbers reported in the paper we use 10,000 replications and a fixed
seed.

`r tabs_r("kd1")`

```{r kd1, cache = TRUE}
ceo.sensitivity <- bootmakr(formula = ceo.model,
                            data = chen, treat = "owner_ceo",
                            benchmark_covariates = "ceo_tenure",
                            cluster = "co_code",
                            reps = 10000, seed = 912323, progress = FALSE)
ceo.sensitivity
```

(`progress = FALSE` only switches off the progress bar, which is useful on
screen but not in a document.)

`r tabs_stata("kd1")`

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("ceo-pay-1", "kd1")
```

`r tabs_end()`

```{r kd1-numbers, include = FALSE}
r1  <- ceo.sensitivity$results
st1 <- stata_results("ceo-pay-1")
s1  <- st1[st1$label == "kd1", ]
ob  <- ceo.sensitivity$benchmark_strength$observed
im  <- ceo.sensitivity$benchmark_strength$implied
```

The first table holds the answer. Had a variable as strong as CEO tenure been
omitted, the coefficient of family CEO would be `r num(r1$estimate)` instead of
`r num(coef(fit)[["owner_ceo"]])`. That point estimate is the one `sensemakr`
reports, and it is the same in R and Stata. What `bootmakr` adds is the
inference: a bootstrap standard error of `r num(s1$se)`, a percentile 95
percent confidence interval from `r num(s1$ci_lower)` to `r num(s1$ci_upper)`,
and a bootstrap *p*-value of `r num(s1$p, 2, lead = FALSE)` in Stata
(`r num(r1$pvalue, 2, lead = FALSE)` in R, with an interval from
`r num(r1$ci_lower)` to `r num(r1$ci_upper)`). The two programs draw different
random numbers, so their bootstrap results agree up to simulation error. The
published effect would therefore no longer be statistically significant at
the 5 percent level if an omitted variable as strong as CEO tenure were at
work.

The second block says what "as strong as CEO tenure" means. CEO tenure
explains `r num(100 * ob$r2dxj.x, 1)` percent of the variance of family-CEO
status that the other covariates leave unexplained (a partial correlation of
`r num(ob$r_dxj.x, 2, lead = FALSE)`), and `r num(100 * ob$r2yxj.dx, 1)`
percent of the remaining variance of log CEO pay (a partial correlation of
`r num(ob$r_yxj.dx, 2, lead = FALSE)`). The omitted variable assumed at
`kd = 1` has an impact of `r num(im$impact, 2, lead = FALSE)`: the product of
its partial correlations with CEO pay and with family-CEO status, on the
scale on which the ITCV is stated. These are descriptive statistics of the
data. No standard error enters them, so they do not depend on whether the
model is estimated with conventional or clustered standard errors, and they
are the same in every bootstrap run. The article
[How strong is the benchmark?](benchmark-strength.html) explains the block in
detail.

### An omitted variable half as strong

Is the conclusion equally fragile for a weaker omitted variable? Set `kd` to
0.5.

`r tabs_r("kd05")`

```{r kd05, cache = TRUE}
half <- bootmakr(ceo.model, data = chen, treat = "owner_ceo",
                 benchmark_covariates = "ceo_tenure", kd = 0.5,
                 cluster = "co_code",
                 reps = 10000, seed = 912323, progress = FALSE)
half
```

`r tabs_stata("kd05")`

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("ceo-pay-1", "kd05")
```

`r tabs_end()`

```{r kd05-numbers, include = FALSE}
r05 <- half$results
s05 <- st1[st1$label == "kd05", ]
```

An omitted variable half as strong as CEO tenure leaves the conclusion intact:
the adjusted coefficient is `r num(r05$estimate)`, with a confidence interval
from `r num(s05$ci_lower)` to `r num(s05$ci_upper)` and *p* =
`r num(s05$p, 2, lead = FALSE)` in Stata (`r num(r05$ci_lower)` to
`r num(r05$ci_upper)` and *p* = `r num(r05$pvalue, 3, lead = FALSE)` in R). The
finding of Chen et al. (2021) withstands moderate omitted-variable bias, but
not an omitted variable as strong as CEO tenure itself.

### The breakdown point

Instead of trying one strength at a time, give `kd` a range of values.
`bootmakr` then reports one row per value, and the plot shows where the
adjusted estimate stops being statistically significant: the *breakdown
point*. With CEO tenure the result already breaks down between `kd = 0.5` and
`kd = 1`, which leaves little to plot, so we take a weaker benchmark, firm
age, and let `kd` run from 0.5 to 4.

`r tabs_r("sweep")`

```{r sweep, cache = TRUE}
breakdown <- bootmakr(ceo.model, data = chen, treat = "owner_ceo",
                      benchmark_covariates = "firm_age",
                      kd = seq(0.5, 4, by = 0.5),
                      cluster = "co_code",
                      reps = 10000, seed = 912323, progress = FALSE)
breakdown
```

```{r sweep-plot, fig.cap = "Bias-adjusted effect of family CEO with bootstrap 95 percent confidence intervals, for an omitted variable 0.5 to 4 times as strong as firm age (R)."}
plot(breakdown, type = "kd_sweep")
```

`r tabs_stata("sweep")`

The `plot` option draws the figure.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("ceo-pay-2", "sweep")
```

```{r sweep-stata-plot, echo = FALSE, fig.cap = "Bias-adjusted effect of family CEO with bootstrap 95 percent confidence intervals, for an omitted variable 0.5 to 4 times as strong as firm age (Stata)."}
knitr::include_graphics(stata_graph("ceo-pay-sweep.svg"), error = FALSE)
```

`r tabs_end()`

```{r sweep-numbers, include = FALSE}
rs  <- breakdown$results
st2 <- stata_results("ceo-pay-2")
ss  <- st2[st2$label == "sweep", ]
ims <- breakdown$benchmark_strength$implied
at  <- function(d, k, col) d[[col]][abs(d$kd - k) < 1e-8]
```

Solid markers are significant at the 5 percent level, hollow markers are not.
The adjusted estimate declines only gently, from `r num(at(rs, 0.5, "estimate"))`
to `r num(at(rs, 4, "estimate"))`, while its confidence interval widens. The
breakdown point is at about 2.5: an omitted variable would have to be roughly
two and a half times as strong as firm age to make the effect statistically
insignificant (*p* = `r num(at(ss, 2.5, "p"), 3, lead = FALSE)` in Stata and
`r num(at(rs, 2.5, "pvalue"), 3, lead = FALSE)` in R at `kd = 2.5`). In terms
of the benchmark-strength block, that is a variable with a partial
R^2^ of about `r num(at(ims, 2.5, "r2dz.x"), 2, lead = FALSE)` with family-CEO
status and `r num(at(ims, 2.5, "r2yz.dx"), 3, lead = FALSE)` with CEO pay, an
impact of `r num(at(ims, 2.5, "impact"), 2, lead = FALSE)` on the ITCV
scale.

### A group of covariates as benchmark

The benchmark can also be a group of covariates taken together, here CEO
tenure and CEO education. Because the two are jointly stronger than either
one alone, smaller multiples are of interest.

`r tabs_r("grouped")`

```{r grouped, cache = TRUE}
grouped <- bootmakr(ceo.model, data = chen, treat = "owner_ceo",
                    gbenchmark_covariates = c("ceo_tenure", "ceo_edu_dummy"),
                    kd = seq(0.1, 0.5, by = 0.1),
                    cluster = "co_code",
                    reps = 10000, seed = 912323, progress = FALSE)
grouped
```

`r tabs_stata("grouped")`

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("ceo-pay-2", "grouped")
```

`r tabs_end()`

```{r grouped-numbers, include = FALSE}
rg  <- grouped$results
sg  <- st2[st2$label == "grouped", ]
obg <- grouped$benchmark_strength$observed
g.last  <- max(sg$kd[sg$p < 0.05])
g.first <- min(sg$kd[sg$p >= 0.05])
```

Together, CEO tenure and CEO education explain
`r num(100 * obg$r2dxj.x, 1)` percent of the remaining variance of
family-CEO status and `r num(100 * obg$r2yxj.dx, 1)` percent of that of CEO
pay. Against this stronger yardstick the effect remains statistically
significant for an omitted variable up to `r g.last` times as strong as the
two together and no longer at `r g.first` times (*p* =
`r num(at(sg, g.first, "p"), 3, lead = FALSE)` in Stata,
`r num(at(rg, g.first, "pvalue"), 3, lead = FALSE)` in R).

## Stage 4: can the bootstrap be trusted?

A bootstrap result is only as good as the bootstrap behind it. Three checks
are worth making.

**Enough clusters.** The cluster bootstrap resamples firms, and it needs
enough of them. Here there are 277, with up to ten observations each, which
the simulations in Lonati and Wulff (2026) suggest is sufficient.

**No failed replications.** Some bootstrap samples can make the assumed
omitted variable impossible, in the sense that it would have to explain more
variance than is left. Such replications fail and are dropped, and `bootmakr`
says so below the table. There is no such note in any of the runs above. A
handful of failures is harmless; many of them mean that the assumed strength
is close to the limit of what the data allow.

**Enough replications.** The headline result sits close to the 5 percent
threshold, so the number of replications matters. Run the default call (1,000
replications) with four different seeds:

`r tabs_r("seeds")`

```{r seeds, cache = TRUE}
seeds <- c(101, 202, 303, 404)

runs <- lapply(seeds, function(s) {
  bootmakr(ceo.model, data = chen, treat = "owner_ceo",
           benchmark_covariates = "ceo_tenure", cluster = "co_code",
           seed = s, progress = FALSE)$results
})

cbind(seed = seeds, do.call(rbind, runs)[, c("pvalue", "ci_lower", "ci_upper")])
```

`r tabs_stata("seeds")`

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("ceo-pay-1", "seeds")
```

`r tabs_end()`

```{r seeds-numbers, include = FALSE}
p.r     <- vapply(runs, function(r) r$pvalue, numeric(1))
p.stata <- as.numeric(sub(".*p = ([0-9.]+).*", "\\1",
                          grep("^seed", stata_output("ceo-pay-1", "seeds"), value = TRUE)))
straddle <- any(c(p.r, p.stata) < 0.05) && any(c(p.r, p.stata) >= 0.05)
if (!straddle && !.pending()) stop("The seed runs no longer straddle .05: revise the next paragraph.")
```

With 1,000 replications the *p*-value changes from seed to seed: between
`r num(min(p.r), 3, lead = FALSE)` and `r num(max(p.r), 3, lead = FALSE)` in
these R runs, and between `r num(min(p.stata), 3, lead = FALSE)` and
`r num(max(p.stata), 3, lead = FALSE)` in the Stata runs. The differences are
small, but the eight values lie on both sides of .05, so the verdict would
depend on the seed. The `converge` option shows how many replications it
takes for the result to settle. It runs the bootstrap once, here with 40,000
replications, and recomputes the standard error and the *p*-value on the
first 1,000, 2,000, ... replications.

`r tabs_r("converge")`

```{r converge, cache = TRUE}
check <- bootmakr(ceo.model, data = chen, treat = "owner_ceo",
                  benchmark_covariates = "ceo_tenure", cluster = "co_code",
                  reps = 40000, seed = 912323, progress = FALSE,
                  converge = list(minreps = 1000, stepsize = 1000,
                                  threshold = 10000))
check
```

```{r converge-plot, fig.height = 7.5, fig.cap = "Bootstrap distribution of the adjusted estimate (top), and its standard error (middle) and p-value (bottom) as the number of replications grows (R)."}
plot(check, type = "convergence")
```

`r tabs_stata("converge")`

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("ceo-pay-3", "converge")
```

```{r converge-stata-plot, echo = FALSE, fig.cap = "Bootstrap distribution of the adjusted estimate (top), and its standard error (middle) and p-value (bottom) as the number of replications grows (Stata)."}
knitr::include_graphics(stata_graph("ceo-pay-converge.svg"), error = FALSE)
```

`r tabs_end()`

```{r converge-numbers, include = FALSE}
cv  <- check$convergence$summary
cvs <- utils::read.delim(file.path(.pc_dir(), "stata", "output", "ceo-pay-3-convergence.txt"))
```

The table below the results summarizes the 40 checkpoints: the mean, the
range and the coefficient of variation of the standard error and of the
*p*-value, first across all checkpoints and then from the threshold of 10,000
replications onwards. In the Stata run the standard error varies by
`r num(cvs$se_range, 4, lead = FALSE)` over all checkpoints, a coefficient of
variation of `r num(cvs$se_cv, 2)` percent: it is stable from the outset.
The *p*-value moves by `r num(cvs$p_range, 3, lead = FALSE)` overall and by
`r num(cvs$p_range_high, 3, lead = FALSE)` once 10,000 or more replications
are used. The R run tells the same story
(`r num(cv$se_range, 4, lead = FALSE)`, `r num(cv$se_cv, 2)` percent,
`r num(cv$p_range, 3, lead = FALSE)` and
`r num(cv$p_range_hi, 3, lead = FALSE)`). The histogram is unimodal and well
behaved. Inference based on 10,000 replications is therefore credible here,
and the verdict stands: an omitted variable as strong as CEO tenure would
leave the effect of family CEO positive but no longer statistically
significant at the 5 percent level.

## What to report

A report of this analysis would state

- the benchmark and why it was chosen (here CEO tenure, a substantively
  meaningful covariate that is closely tied to who becomes a family CEO), and
  its strength in partial R^2^ terms;
- the multiples `kd` examined, and for each the bias-adjusted coefficient with
  its bootstrap confidence interval and *p*-value;
- how the bootstrap was run: the resampling unit (firms), the number of
  replications, the seed, and the number of failed replications;
- the outcome of the convergence check if the verdict is close to the
  significance threshold.

For example: *Assuming an omitted variable as strong as CEO tenure, the
bias-adjusted effect of family CEO on log CEO pay is
`r num(r1$estimate)` (cluster bootstrap with 10,000 replications; percentile
95% CI [`r num(s1$ci_lower)`, `r num(s1$ci_upper)`], p =
`r num(s1$p, 2, lead = FALSE)`).*

A sensitivity analysis does not replace a research design that rules out
omitted variables. It makes explicit how much unobserved confounding a
conclusion can take, and it leaves the judgment of whether that much is
plausible to the reader.

## Notes on reproducibility

The R results on this page were produced with R `r getRversion()`,
`bootmakr` `r packageVersion("bootmakr")` and `sensemakr`
`r packageVersion("sensemakr")`; the Stata results with StataNow 19.5,
`bootmakr` 1.2.0 and the `sensemakr` command from SSC. With the seed shown,
each program reproduces its own numbers exactly. On an ordinary laptop, 1,000
replications of this model take about 20 seconds in R and 45 seconds in
Stata, and 10,000 replications under 4 and 8 minutes.

## References

Chen, G., Chittoor, R., & Vissa, B. (2021). Does nepotism run in the family?
CEO pay and pay-performance sensitivity in Indian family firms. *Strategic
Management Journal*, 42(7), 1326--1343. <https://doi.org/10.1002/smj.3263>

Cinelli, C., & Hazlett, C. (2020). Making sense of sensitivity: Extending
omitted variable bias. *Journal of the Royal Statistical Society: Series B*,
82(1), 39--67. <https://doi.org/10.1111/rssb.12348>

Cinelli, C., Ferwerda, J., & Hazlett, C. (2024). sensemakr: Sensitivity
analysis tools for OLS in R and Stata. *Observational Studies*, 10(2),
93--127. <https://doi.org/10.1353/obs.2024.a946583>

Lonati, S., & Wulff, J. N. (2026). Why you should not use the ITCV with
robust standard errors (and what to do instead). *Academy of Management
Proceedings*, 2026(1). <https://doi.org/10.5465/AMPROC.2026.247bp>
